  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 AM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nEarly this morning, water vapor reveals a weak midlevel trough \ndropping southeast into the Great Lakes, while a well-defined \nmidlevel low spins over the Rockies. This northern trough is \nbringing with it another shot of CAA, with 850mb temperatures \nalready falling to near 0C. Nighttime microphysics shows extensive \nlower-level cloud cover across the region, in spite of drier \nmidlevels, perhaps due in part to this wave moving through as well \nas lingering lower-level moisture in the wake of yesterday's \nrainfall. This is helping to keep temperatures at least a few \ndegrees warmer than previously expected, with temperatures still \nonly in the mid 40s for most expected to fall just slightly further \ninto the lower to mid 40s area-wide. Heading into the daytime hours, \nthe shortwave moves out, to be replaced by a brief period of \nmidlevel ridging and surface high pressure. However, only partially \nclearing skies are expected as the cooler airmass over the lake may \nbe just enough to support lingering lake clouds. Otherwise, expect \nhighs ranging in the 50s with breezy conditions at least across the \neastern UP; there, NW winds may continue to gust to near 20mph \nthrough the afternoon. Winds go light tonight while temperatures \nplummet into the lower 30s and even the upper 20s in the interior-\nwest. If more breaks in the cloud cover occur, these cooler \ntemperatures may become more widespread. \n\nThe main concern of this forecast period continues to be the \nevolution of a surface low in the Great Lakes driven by two phasing \ntroughs Thursday night through Friday. Operational deterministic \nmodels and their ensemble counterparts are in pretty good agreement \nthat a ~1009 mb surface low will be placed in the vicinity of \nsouthern Lake Michigan by Thursday evening. Ample northward moisture \ntransport and isentropic ascent ahead of the lead northern stream \ntrough will provide enough support for widespread rain lifting into \nthe UP Thursday afternoon through Friday morning and latest NBM \nsuggests at least a 50-80% chance for QPF >0.25\". Early Friday \nmorning through Fri afternoon the surface low feature is slated to \nwork northeast atop the Lower Lakes, placing Upper Michigan on the \nnorthwestern flank. Strong cold air advection and rapid pressure \nrises (upwards of 1.5-2mb/hr) will contribute to northerly wind \nspeeds reaching 25-35 kts gusting to near 50 kts on Lake Superior \nand the adjacent lakeshores. There is some uncertainty regarding the \nmaximum gust potential as ensembles (NBM and ENS) have moderate to \nhigh probability (50-70% chance) of gusts >50 kts whereas model \nsoundings reveal peak mixing largely near 45 kts. Lake \neffect/enhanced rain on the backside of the system will provide an \nadditional 0.1-0.25\" of precip once system rain clear the area. \nWouldn't surprise me to see some wet snow mix into areas of the \ninterior highlands early Friday morning before temperature warm into \nthe 40s Friday afternoon. No accumulations expected. As winds settle \nlate Friday and dry high pressure clears skies from west to east, \ntemperatures will drop like a rock overnight into Saturday morning, \nreaching the mid to low 20s for much if not all of the interior \nwhile the lakeshores may reach freezing for the first time this \nfall. Ridging and dry weather dominate through the rest of the \nweekend with the potential for another warm up / fire weather \npattern Sunday into Monday.",
